{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic","id":1300186,"title":"Technocracy : spontaneous infrastructure or technocratic magic?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-03-26T20:41:28-07:00","id":1300186,"title":"Technocracy : spontaneous infrastructure or technocratic magic?","content":"Hello,<br /> <br /> I have revisiting a lot of technocracy books (and reading Panopticon Quest) as of late and I have reached a aspect of technocratic procedures that I have considered many times but I do not fully understand. For a technocrat to preform 'magic' they must be an enlightened individual with a technocratic paradigm, which traditionally implies that they are a scientist or engineer or some other educated background that understands science and believes in it. As far as I understand though a mage doesn't need to have a high intelligence or wit score to be enlightened, and it is implied that technocrats are not all geniuses with a omniscience background. This doesn't stop an individual from accessing other union technology however, a NWO agent with life spheres and low intelligence can still use a stim pack as a focus to boost their physical strength or a damage control agent from the progenitors can fire a phase rifle straight from Iteration X that they can just magically use/ somehow have. In these cases an individual is accessing tech they don't understand or is not their particular focus, they might be a highly studious character or soldier with limited background, but if they have the spheres and the enlightenment they can still create a bevy of effects. <br /> <br /> Other times it seems that technocrats can spontaneously create effects that are retroactive through &quot;training&quot; or other efforts they seemingly did with no actual prep-work that is discussed until the exact time where the effect is needed. A syndicate enforcer can use a detonator as a focus for a forces procedure to spontaneously detonate C4 explosives that were never actually planted, or a void engineer can say that he activates his armor's psychic shielding and use that as a mind to resist psychic assaults.<br /> <br /> My point is that the technocratic paradigm becomes incredibly confusing at times because there is an unclear line drawn with whether effects are actually preformed by these individuals or if its the technocratic paradigm in action. Does my NWO agent need to actually plant a hidden weapon in their coat every morning to make it appear with the holdout weapon procedure. Does my space marine need to have an in depth knowledge of their power armor to modify its technology and create these effects? Or do they just believe that its shielding that is spontaneously has will do the trick and block out psychic interference. Does enlightenment actually translate to an understanding of technology that is purely academic or is it more of a belief that tech will work for you?<br /> <br /> Or am I just a silly idiot and is this how people justify spontaneous effects in game? <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> This might be a silly question of semantics, but I think about it almost every time a procedure comes up in the convention books. I think my explanation of my question might be a little roundabout but the core question is 'does enlightenment represent a technocrats ability to create effects based their own understanding of of reality, or is it their enlightened will that allows them spontaneously change technology to create effects and procedures?'<br /> <br /> Let me know what you think of my big mess<br /> -Brassfist","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-27T05:49:09-07:00","id":1300246,"title":"","content":"First off, it is right to feel confused in this situation. Example magical effects are frequently treated differently depending on who is writing them. This has led to more than a few discussions... and arguments... and flame wars (in Mage's younger days).<br /> <br /> I can't give you a direct answer because you are actually touching on multiple different issues. Instead let me share a few subjects you are touching on that might help you figure out how to handle this:<br /> <br /> The first is the rules for skills modifying magic rules. They are somewhat optional, but they are meant to reflect the learning, experience, and actions that a practitioner of a certain kind of magic might need to take. They are there to show the differences between two Mages of equal spheres and unequal intelligence/learning. That's one way of representing the difference between the inventor of power armor and the space marine who uses it.<br /> <br /> The second is that while the player always knows when their character is casting, their character does not. There are scenarios where Technocracy agents are making things happen (usually malfunctions or collapses) but they aren't aware that they are. That's where some of the perception that the Technocracy is spontaneously casting are coming from. <br /> <br /> Frankly, spontaneously casting is a very important thing and the Technocracy would consistently be at a major disadvantage if they didn't have a few ways of doing it, so there have been multiple attempts to set up ways that they can. The Syndicate and NWO especially are known for embedding useful things in regular technology so that they have more abilities in urban areas. I wouldn't go so far as to say they plant actual explosives in factory made fuse boxes, but I would expect there are things hidden in regular fuse boxes that make them easy to overload, or in antennae that make them easy to hijack.<br /> <br /> Fourth, one of the powerful elements of the Technocracy is that they are more standardized in paradigm than the Traditions are. It makes sense that members of the Conventions are able to use each other's basic tech even if it is not their primary foci. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-27T16:55:19-07:00","id":1300418,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300246#post1300246\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> The second is that while the player always knows when their character is casting, their character does not. There are scenarios where Technocracy agents are making things happen (usually malfunctions or collapses) but they aren't aware that they are. That's where some of the perception that the Technocracy is spontaneously casting are coming from.<br /> <br /> Frankly, spontaneously casting is a very important thing and the Technocracy would consistently be at a major disadvantage if they didn't have a few ways of doing it, so there have been multiple attempts to set up ways that they can. The Syndicate and NWO especially are known for embedding useful things in regular technology so that they have more abilities in urban areas. I wouldn't go so far as to say they plant actual explosives in factory made fuse boxes, but I would expect there are things hidden in regular fuse boxes that make them easy to overload, or in antennae that make them easy to hijack.<br /> <br /> Fourth, one of the powerful elements of the Technocracy is that they are more standardized in paradigm than the Traditions are. It makes sense that members of the Conventions are able to use each other's basic tech even if it is not their primary foci.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>So I guess to narrow my question a bit I would like to know how far an agents ability to create spontaneous effects can go?<br /> <br /> I understand this is partly an interpretation of the rules as well as how the game is actually played but I struggle to understand if hidden features need to be fully justified/plausible in their existence or if they are actually created spontaneously when an agent needs one. It is one thing to cause a roof to collapse by firing at structural weakness, this is a coincidence and is completely plausible for a technocrat to do. It is an entirely different thing to for a syndicate enforcer to pick up a kitchen knife and with a high enough matter sphere transform it because it has hidden features. From my understanding you are saying this kind of effect counts as spontaneous, but in actuality it still has to be actually implemented by the technocracy ahead of time. <br /> <br /> So where I am stuck is whether or not these features actually exist in universe with full justification; Are they <i>actually</i> created ahead of time in a syndicate factory, shipped to the masses, used on a global scale, and when an agent runs into some random sleeper home in a desperate scramble to escape werewolves notices this product with hidden features? Or is it that a technocrats enlightenment allows them to look at mundane objects that normally do not have hyper tech in them and say, &quot;that is a syndicate product&quot; and cause the effect because they are altering reality with their enlightened will.<br /> <br /> Where does it stop being real technology and when does it turn into reality warping?<br /> <br /> If I am unclear in my question let me know and I can try and reword it again, I feel like I am a little more on point this time.<br /> <br /> -Brassfist <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T05:29:08-07:00","id":1300520,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300418#post1300418\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So I guess to narrow my question a bit I would like to know how far an agents ability to create spontaneous effects can go?<br /> <br /> I understand this is partly an interpretation of the rules as well as how the game is actually played but I struggle to understand if hidden features need to be fully justified/plausible in their existence or if they are actually created spontaneously when an agent needs one. It is one thing to cause a roof to collapse by firing at structural weakness, this is a coincidence and is completely plausible for a technocrat to do. It is an entirely different thing to for a syndicate enforcer to pick up a kitchen knife and with a high enough matter sphere transform it because it has hidden features. From my understanding you are saying this kind of effect counts as spontaneous, but in actuality it still has to be actually implemented by the technocracy ahead of time.</div> </div> </div> </div>Some of this is just a matter of the Storyteller and Players crafting a good story. If it makes sense that there could be something useful to the technocrat hidden in the scene and it makes the story better, then it is there. It's not a matter of magic and has nothing to do with the rules, it is just the ebb and flow of storytelling* The two major caveats is that this kind of storytelling has to work for the group in question and the thing assumed has to be reasonable. <br /> <br /> The Technocracy is not seeding the kitchen knife industry with secret features. Kitchen knives aren't modifiable enough in the public consciousness and the circumstances that kind of feature be useful are limited. A technocrat could still use matter to transform the knife, but his tool could not be that the knife was already set up to do that. He'd have to have another way of making that work. Breaker boxes, cameras, cell towers, and cars are everywhere though. Having subtle things built into a lot (but not all, they aren't that powerful) of them is more plausible.<br /> <br /> <br /> *As a non mage example, the last time I DMd D&amp;D I would frequently tell my players that they didn't have to ask me about every object that was in a scene, they could assume the presence of some things as long as they were reasonable. They could assume the chemicals in an alchemy lab were flammable. They could assume that a table in a mine had a random assortment of broken gears to throw at people. They could even assume that rarer things were present so long as they didn't abuse the privilege, and I could do the same.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So where I am stuck is whether or not these features actually exist in universe with full justification; Are they <i>actually</i> created ahead of time in a syndicate factory, shipped to the masses, used on a global scale, and when an agent runs into some random sleeper home in a desperate scramble to escape werewolves notices this product with hidden features? Or is it that a technocrats enlightenment allows them to look at mundane objects that normally do not have hyper tech in them and say, &quot;that is a syndicate product&quot; and cause the effect because they are altering reality with their enlightened will.<br /> <br /> Where does it stop being real technology and when does it turn into reality warping?<br /> <br /> If I am unclear in my question let me know and I can try and reword it again, I feel like I am a little more on point this time.<br /> <br /> -Brassfist<br /> <br /> </div> </div> </div>If the spontaneous casting relies on hidden features, then at least within the fiction it must be set up ahead of time. It exists, in universe, with justification. If a technocrat uses an embedded feature in an off the shelf camera to, I don't know, activate a secret facial recognition program then that embedded feature was always there. He didn't unconsciously add it at the moment he picked it up, he had foreknowledge that it was there. If he was unlucky, and the camera he grabbed off the shelf didn't have that feature (because even the Technocracy can't seed every camera) then he couldn't use the effect without consciously modifying the camera itself. <br /> <br /> Out of fiction, some groups can get away with adding things to the scene that make sense even if the ST didn't know they were there when the scene started. Some also roll a die to determine if a fortunate piece of seeded tech happens to be in the scene. Both of these cases assume the elements were actually always there even if they hadn't been seen previously. Also neither of these has anything to do with the <i>characters</i>. The characters aren't doing anything, the players are just deciding what makes sense in fiction.<br /> <br /> Now two things to keep in mind. There are no syndicate factories that are churning out products for the public. If the Technocracy is seeding technology they are doing it by using bribery or corporate espionage to get their small changes into sleeper created parts. That sets a limit on how ridiculous the seeded tech can be. A TV will not transform into a robot, the modifications would be too obvious. It might be able to record, or think, or explode like a console on a Star Trek set. It also sets a range on how prevalent the tech could be. It is produced on a global scale, but it is hard for even the technocracy to reach every company and every factory.<br /> <br /> Second, none of this applies to the <i>other</i> way the technocrats spontaneously cast, which is by observing facts about the world. Earlier you used the idea of a Syndicate Agent determining where to shoot to collapse a roof. Well in the right circumstances he doesn't have to shoot. If the firefight (or some other thing that could cause observable damage) has already happened he can 'calculate' that it is about to collapse in 5...4...3...2...1... In that case the roof was not about to collapse and the player is absolutely making an Arete roll to bring the house down. The character may not realize they are doing anything though. <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-28T19:15:14-07:00","id":1300730,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300520#post1300520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Some of this is just a matter of the Storyteller and Players crafting a good story. If it makes sense that there could be something useful to the technocrat hidden in the scene and it makes the story better, then it is there. It's not a matter of magic and has nothing to do with the rules, it is just the ebb and flow of storytelling* The two major caveats is that this kind of storytelling has to work for the group in question and the thing assumed has to be reasonable.<br /> <br /> The Technocracy is not seeding the kitchen knife industry with secret features. Kitchen knives aren't modifiable enough in the public consciousness and the circumstances that kind of feature be useful are limited. A technocrat could still use matter to transform the knife, but his tool could not be that the knife was already set up to do that. He'd have to have another way of making that work. Breaker boxes, cameras, cell towers, and cars are everywhere though. Having subtle things built into a lot (but not all, they aren't that powerful) of them is more plausible.<br /> <br /> <br /> *As a non mage example, the last time I DMd D&amp;D I would frequently tell my players that they didn't have to ask me about every object that was in a scene, they could assume the presence of some things as long as they were reasonable. They could assume the chemicals in an alchemy lab were flammable. They could assume that a table in a mine had a random assortment of broken gears to throw at people. They could even assume that rarer things were present so long as they didn't abuse the privilege, and I could do the same.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> If the spontaneous casting relies on hidden features, then at least within the fiction it must be set up ahead of time. It exists, in universe, with justification. If a technocrat uses an embedded feature in an off the shelf camera to, I don't know, activate a secret facial recognition program then that embedded feature was always there. He didn't unconsciously add it at the moment he picked it up, he had foreknowledge that it was there. If he was unlucky, and the camera he grabbed off the shelf didn't have that feature (because even the Technocracy can't seed every camera) then he couldn't use the effect without consciously modifying the camera itself.<br /> <br /> Out of fiction, some groups can get away with adding things to the scene that make sense even if the ST didn't know they were there when the scene started. Some also roll a die to determine if a fortunate piece of seeded tech happens to be in the scene. Both of these cases assume the elements were actually always there even if they hadn't been seen previously. Also neither of these has anything to do with the <i>characters</i>. The characters aren't doing anything, the players are just deciding what makes sense in fiction.<br /> <br /> Now two things to keep in mind. There are no syndicate factories that are churning out products for the public. If the Technocracy is seeding technology they are doing it by using bribery or corporate espionage to get their small changes into sleeper created parts. That sets a limit on how ridiculous the seeded tech can be. A TV will not transform into a robot, the modifications would be too obvious. It might be able to record, or think, or explode like a console on a Star Trek set. It also sets a range on how prevalent the tech could be. It is produced on a global scale, but it is hard for even the technocracy to reach every company and every factory.<br /> <br /> Second, none of this applies to the <i>other</i> way the technocrats spontaneously cast, which is by observing facts about the world. Earlier you used the idea of a Syndicate Agent determining where to shoot to collapse a roof. Well in the right circumstances he doesn't have to shoot. If the firefight (or some other thing that could cause observable damage) has already happened he can 'calculate' that it is about to collapse in 5...4...3...2...1... In that case the roof was not about to collapse and the player is absolutely making an Arete roll to bring the house down. The character may not realize they are doing anything though.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Makes sense to me, thanks for helping me understand!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T03:05:00-07:00","id":1300786,"title":"","content":"It's sometimes worth simply boiling it down to Vulgar or Coincidental when it comes to this sort of thing; one of the features listed as something to consider with Vulgar/Coincidental is Resources. Are you working with something that already exists (the taxi down the street) or something that didn't exist (a taxi you create out of thin air).<br /> <br /> If a Technocrat's explanation for blowing up a lightbulb to inflict a huge amount of damage to a building is &quot;well it has secret technological features&quot;, that's bullshit. It's just made up. That's Vulgar, simply because the internal explanation doesn't even make full sense within the Mage's paradigm; a lightbulb shattering could be coincidental, but the Mage's own internal justifications have to make sense. Just in the way that someone could just happen to develop a cold, but an Akashayana couldn't infect them by sending them an email, since that's not how their magick works, and they don't believe in it. Instead the Technocrat should actually use their Paradigm (worth remembering that while the whole union has highly standardized and formatted Practice, which makes it very easy to work together, personal Paradigm will vary, from Tech Holds All Answers to Might Makes Right and Everything is Data) to determine how they make the lightbulb explode.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well by tweaking the wavelengths traveling through space around me using my personal disruption gear, I can concentrate a lattice of electromagnetic energy around the lightbulb to blow it up&quot;. Simple. Much better than pretending something always was a certain way.<br /> <br /> This problem only really seems to come up with Mages who aren't proper scientists in and of themselves; black suits who are the agents of the technocracy. In this case you simply have to remember that NOT ALL MAGES CAN DO EVERYTHING. Sometimes Technocrats are at a significant disadvantage because they simply can't justify blowing something up or conjuring fire, they are limited to scaring people with suit/sunglasses and Mind, blasting people with a gun with forces, finding the perfect part of a building to destroy to bring the whole thing down with Matter/Entropy... you get the idea. Some Mages are limited. Some are stuck at their desk with every effect taking 5 hours because to them alchemy is this slow process. And that's fine. If you don't want your mage to be like that, design a Mage with a paradigm/practice that allows improvisation. There's a reason that Techgnosi / Scientific Mystic is an expensive merit. There are huge benefits and drawbacks to both technomancy and mysticism. Having the benefits of both is powerful.<br /> <br /> So replace your &quot;I can do stuff with my gun, mirrorshades, fake IDs and car&quot; NWO agent with a &quot;I have latent psionic abilities, and they aren't deviant because it's all been signed off as perfectly scientific and rational&quot; NWO agent. Same aesthetic, same Spheres, same cool concepts, but you now have an actual justification for bullshitting things like turning spoons into throwing knives. After all, your psionic powers can do all sorts of weird things, just expect nosebleeds and hallucinations if you overdo it, thanks to inverse psychic feedback (Paradox). And while your powers are rational, they are way ahead of The Timetable! The Technocracy want you to be discreet with them; throwing things around with your mind in front of the masses is highly unmutual.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-29-2019, 08:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T17:11:38-07:00","id":1300983,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300786#post1300786\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's sometimes worth simply boiling it down to Vulgar or Coincidental when it comes to this sort of thing; one of the features listed as something to consider with Vulgar/Coincidental is Resources. Are you working with something that already exists (the taxi down the street) or something that didn't exist (a taxi you create out of thin air).<br /> <br /> If a Technocrat's explanation for blowing up a lightbulb to inflict a huge amount of damage to a building is &quot;well it has secret technological features&quot;, that's bullshit. It's just made up. That's Vulgar, simply because the internal explanation doesn't even make full sense within the Mage's paradigm; a lightbulb shattering could be coincidental, but the Mage's own internal justifications have to make sense. Just in the way that someone could just happen to develop a cold, but an Akashayana couldn't infect them by sending them an email, since that's not how their magick works, and they don't believe in it.. Instead the Technocrat should actually use their Paradigm (worth remembering that while the whole union has highly standardized and formatted Practice, which makes it very easy to work together, personal Paradigm will vary, from Tech Holds All Answers to Might Makes Right and Everything is Data) to determine how they make the lightbulb explode.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well by tweaking the wavelengths traveling through space around me using my personal disruption gear, I can concentrate a lattice of electromagnetic energy around the lightbulb to blow it up&quot;. Simple. Much better than pretending something always was a certain way.<br /> <br /> This problem only really seems to come up with Mages who aren't proper scientists in and of themselves; black suits who are the agents of the technocracy. In this case you simply have to remember that NOT ALL MAGES CAN DO EVERYTHING. Sometimes Technocrats are at a significant disadvantage because they simply can't justify blowing something up or conjuring fire, they are limited to scaring people with suit/sunglasses and Mind, blasting people with a gun with forces, finding the perfect part of a building to destroy to bring the whole thing down with Matter/Entropy... you get the idea. Some Mages are limited. Some are stuck at their desk with every effect taking 5 hours because to them alchemy is this slow process. And that's fine. If you don't want your mage to be like that, design a Mage with a paradigm/practice that allows improvisation. There's a reason that Techgnosi / Scientific Mystic is an expensive merit. There are huge benefits and drawbacks to both technomancy and mysticism. Having the benefits of both is powerful.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this was a big part of my problem when talking about mage. I always want every paradigm to be able to achieve <i>everything</i> on its own with no outside help. And for the most part a lot of technocrats can achieve most effects, but for a syndicate magic man or a NWO agent that is just not going to be true. They have enlightened combat expertise or manipulative modeling, but they are not going to be able to teleport, shapeshift, or stop time without serious help from an outside resource. I know I can think of ways you can use hyperpsychology and hypereconomics to pull some nonsense effects, but I think its probably better to just stick within the bounds of whats possible and what actually works. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T17:42:27-07:00","id":1300985,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300983#post1300983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this was a big part of my problem when talking about mage. I always want every paradigm to be able to achieve <i>everything</i> on its own with no outside help. And for the most part a lot of technocrats can achieve most effects, but for a syndicate magic man or a NWO agent that is just not going to be true. They have enlightened combat expertise or manipulative modeling, but they are not going to be able to teleport, shapeshift, or stop time without serious help from an outside resource. I know I can think of ways you can use hyperpsychology and hypereconomics to pull some nonsense effects, but I think its probably better to just stick within the bounds of whats possible and what actually works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, a big hurdle is realizing that an effect just isn't always justifiable. Syndicate money men can't recharge their platinum cards at sacred groves, even if they are Nodes. Men in Black can't puppeteer other human beings the way a witch could.<br /> <br /> If a PC wants to play as a Mage who can justify any effect (even if many are made Vulgar), then they're free to do so! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /> That can be incredibly fun. But it's also fun to play a Mage with certain limitations, who believes reality works a certain way and sticks to it quite closely. In that case play up other advantages; how Coincidental your effects are, how well your Backgrounds (Certification for example) back them up, that sort of thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-29T18:57:58-07:00","id":1300998,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300983#post1300983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I think this was a big part of my problem when talking about mage. I always want every paradigm to be able to achieve <i>everything</i> on its own with no outside help. And for the most part a lot of technocrats can achieve most effects, but for a syndicate magic man or a NWO agent that is just not going to be true. They have enlightened combat expertise or manipulative modeling, but they are not going to be able to teleport, shapeshift, or stop time without serious help from an outside resource. I know I can think of ways you can use hyperpsychology and hypereconomics to pull some nonsense effects, but I think its probably better to just stick within the bounds of whats possible and what actually works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Teleport? Shapeshift? Stop time? Aren't all those vulgar as hell? Isn't your syndicate magic man or agent not missing out much?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12952,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","user":"Accelerator","date":". 03-30-2019, 03:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T04:12:56-07:00","id":1301055,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1300983#post1300983\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They have enlightened combat expertise or manipulative modeling, but they are not going to be able to teleport, shapeshift, or stop time without serious help from an outside resource.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's more a matter of actually giving them the proper equipment. Like, if your Syndicate mage has Time 5, you should actually give them an instrument that in some way reflects that sphere. I mean, Book of Secrets has a Syndicate agent who's a cyborg. If he had Time 5, he'd presumably have some special machinery integrated into him.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to push it, you could even use the Technocracy itself as a focus. Like, a Syndicate VP drains the node in a sacred grove by using their rank to bring in machinery to do it.<br /> <br /> I think the underlying issue is that the Technocracy books are pretty conservative in how they present individual NPCs and their magic, but there's material to go pretty wild with if you want.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T12:35:12-07:00","id":1301117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Michael</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301055#post1301055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's more a matter of actually giving them the proper equipment. Like, if your Syndicate mage has Time 5, you should actually give them an instrument that in some way reflects that sphere. I mean, Book of Secrets has a Syndicate agent who's a cyborg. If he had Time 5, he'd presumably have some special machinery integrated into him.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to push it, you could even use the Technocracy itself as a focus. Like, a Syndicate VP drains the node in a sacred grove by using their rank to bring in machinery to do it.<br /> <br /> I think the underlying issue is that the Technocracy books are pretty conservative in how they present individual NPCs and their magic, but there's material to go pretty wild with if you want.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I mean sure I can go super wild with my magic and preform crazy effects, but in regards to my syndicate agent being able to personally create his own devices and fix issues if the device malfunctions....Not really an option. I understand part of the syndicate paradigm is &quot;I am the money guy, I'll buy the solutions to all my woes&quot;, but that shit can put you in a point where you rely on other NPC's and their support which can get dangerous. <br /> <br /> This is entirely a personal opinion, but I think in the business world (I have work in banking and insurance in my life) it is far more important to be a creator than a business man/woman. Being fabulously rich is what the syndicate does, but without the SPD they do not make much as individuals. They are the business people in the TU and as such they work as managers, middlemen, and financiers. Part of the reason why I love them so much is because they are a personal reflection of my own educational path, the interests I used to have when I was in school, and where I currently am trying to reach. On the other hand, I am disgusted by the fact that I have no skills in creating solutions to problems, that I cannot create answers to problems as well as an engineer or scientist or technician, and that I must rely on others to contribute to society and take from them to grow. Kind of a personal rant, and also part of myself that I am trying to change, but it is very difficult to do so. <br /> <br /> Whether i like it or not my PC reflects this mindset, and I find it incredibly terrifying to rely on other individuals in the technocracy because showing weakness or needing favors can set up dangerous power games within the TU. If you can build your own tech then you are totally fine, Just build a new application into your power armor or bio augmentations that let you solve your own problems in the heat of the moment. But needing a specific doctor or engineer to fix your body/intruments when they stop working or when you need a new ability? That comes right back to my question I asked ages ago 'what can an unarmed technocrat actually do. <br /> <br /> The more I talk here the more I realize that I seem to be using the Syndicate as an facsimile for my life and the choices I have made and the disappointment I have with my inability to solve engineer and science problems (childish, I know). If you have any insight on how I can design my syndicate to be more hands on with their solutions that would be great. <br /> <br /> -Brassfist","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T13:49:51-07:00","id":1301141,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I understand part of the syndicate paradigm is &quot;I am the money guy, I'll buy the solutions to all my woes&quot;, but that shit can put you in a point where you rely on other NPC's and their support which can get dangerous. </div> </div> </div>That is more or less the point. All Technocrats do that to varying degrees, it's just more obvious with the Syndicate.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301117#post1301117\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is entirely a personal opinion, but I think in the business world (I have work in banking and insurance in my life) it is far more important to be a creator than a business man/woman.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, they were created to be an <span style=\"font-family:Helvetica, Arial, Verdana, sans-serif\"><span style=\"color:#252c2f\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">antagonistic</span></span></span> faction. They're the self-perception of the masters of the universe and the job-creators taken to the point that they bend reality (at least the powerful higher ups are). They're not meant to reflect reality.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That comes right back to my question I asked ages ago 'what can an unarmed technocrat actually do. </div> </div> </div>Comparatively little, that's the point of them being unable to discard their instruments. I'd keep in mind that mystic mages generally rely on their instruments as well.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T15:00:23-07:00","id":1301159,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301117#post1301117\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">'what can an unarmed technocrat actually do.</div> </div> </div> </div>Much less than an unarmed mystic. A Hermetic will always have Latin chants, a Chorister will always have Hymns, and a Verbena will always have her own blood.<br /> <br /> With that said, a Technocrat will always have access to their own body for Martial Arts, Yoga and similar, and they do use eye contact and speech for some subtle effects. Furthermore, many technocrats are ALWAYS armed thanks to Enhancements. Then you have your Psychics and similar who are exceptions to the rules.<br /> <br /> Basically there are advantages to being a technomancer, but one of the big downsides is that you actually NEED all your gear, and without it you're basically sunk. They're very dependent on instruments and Wonders, to their detriment in cases where they're captured.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T15:41:23-07:00","id":1301172,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301159#post1301159\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Much less than an unarmed mystic. A Hermetic will always have Latin chants, a Chorister will always have Hymns, and a Verbena will always have her own blood.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>This is bullshit. There are always mystics who can't function in the real world. They are just rarer because the world doesn't have a place for them. A Technocratic field agent is built for Field agency. They are James Bond. You take away the cool toys.. he can still wreck your world.<br /> <br /> <br /> So its rather simple. You build a Technocrat who can &quot;work unarmed&quot; not counting the easy ways of cheating(Psychics, bioware, Cyberware) you know how to macguyver shit, you know how the human body works and where to punch someone so they &quot;STAY THE FUCK DOWN&quot; you know how the human mind works and can say afew words and get someone to betray their best friend. Its not Mind control, its just SCIENCE. The Syndicate agent knows all the odds. <br /> <br /> I mean most of the high end adept+master tricks.. require more than 3 seconds and a bit of chanting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T16:41:43-07:00","id":1301186,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301172#post1301172\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I've lost all sense of respect and I'm randomly throwing insults. There are always mystics who can't function in the real world. They are just rarer because the world doesn't have a place for them. A Technocratic field agent is built for Field agency. They are James Bond. You take away the cool toys.. he can still wreck your world. I know that you said that already, but I'm repeating it?</div> </div> </div> </div><i>Much less than an unarmed mystic. A Hermetic will always have Latin chants, a Chorister will always have Hymns, and a Verbena will always have her own blood.<br /> <br /> With that said, a Technocrat will always have<b> access to their own body for Martial Arts</b>, <b>Yoga</b> and similar, and they do use <b>eye contact</b> and <b>speech</b> for some subtle effects. Furthermore, many technocrats are ALWAYS armed thanks to <b>Enhancements</b>. Then you have your <b>Psychics</b> and similar who are exceptions to the rules.</i><br /> <br /> <i>Basically there are advantages to being a technomancer, but one of the big downsides is that you actually NEED all your gear, and without it you're basically sunk. They're very dependent on instruments and Wonders, to their detriment in cases where they're captured.</i><br /> <br /> Cool. Thanks for... reiterating?<br /> <br /> Technocrats are more reliant on their tools than average. Exceptions play an important role.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-30T19:56:56-07:00","id":1301211,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301186#post1301186\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Technocrats are more reliant on their tools than average. Exceptions play an important role.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your understanding of mechanics is flawed and perpetuates a major issue of &quot;Mystics can do whatever they want because Magic I don't have to explain it&quot; but not holding Technocrats to the same standards. A naked technocrat optimized for being a naked Technocrat will be at least as good or better than your Naked mystic. The idea that &quot;They always have chanting/hymns or their blood&quot; is no different than saying &quot;A technocrat always has his training and his cyberware&quot;. <br /> <br /> The only advantage mystics have is easy of surpassing Instrament. That's it. They do not have a greater access to ease of use or have instraments that are more prone to being used in combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-31T01:19:01-07:00","id":1301245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301186#post1301186\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <i>Much less than an unarmed mystic. A Hermetic will always have Latin chants, a Chorister will always have Hymns, and a Verbena will always have her own blood.<br /> <br /> With that said, a Technocrat will always have<b> access to their own body for Martial Arts</b>, <b>Yoga</b> and similar, and they do use <b>eye contact</b> and <b>speech</b> for some subtle effects. Furthermore, many technocrats are ALWAYS armed thanks to <b>Enhancements</b>. Then you have your <b>Psychics</b> and similar who are exceptions to the rules.</i><br /> <br /> <i>Basically there are advantages to being a technomancer, but one of the big downsides is that you actually NEED all your gear, and without it you're basically sunk. They're very dependent on instruments and Wonders, to their detriment in cases where they're captured.</i><br /> <br /> Cool. Thanks for... reiterating?<br /> <br /> Technocrats are more reliant on their tools than average. Exceptions play an important role.</div> </div> </div> </div>Do you think it makes sense to play a syndicate that actually understands and works with products that they create? Like, I\u2019d like to play an entrepreneurial engineer of some sort that still can craft solutions and devices like an iterator or a progenitor when needed? What methodology would that fall into (spd doesn\u2019t count because they are all evil as hell). The NWO has Q division and there are barely any details listed for them, but I could see a macguyver type come from them. I\u2019m having trouble with figuring out what that would look like for the syndicate ?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16848-brassfist","name":"Brassfist","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"39"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-31T02:00:57-07:00","id":1301250,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301245#post1301245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Do you think it makes sense to play a syndicate that actually understands and works with products that they create? Like, I\u2019d like to play an entrepreneurial engineer of some sort that still can craft solutions and devices like an iterator or a progenitor when needed? What methodology would that fall into (spd doesn\u2019t count because they are all evil as hell). The NWO has Q division and there are barely any details listed for them, but I could see a macguyver type come from them. I\u2019m having trouble with figuring out what that would look like for the syndicate ?</div> </div> </div> </div>So your average Syndicate guy can just buy all the tools he needs, requisition them from Q Branch, purchase surplus etc. But that can be boring for a PC who wants to do things themselves.<br /> <br /> With that said, an inventor Syndicate mage is 100% a good concept! There is cross-convention training after all.<br /> <br /> You could play as a former Q Branch engineer who became an business mogul, for example.<br /> <br /> I'd say that the &quot;normal&quot; answer is that a Syndicate Mage with Prime 2 Forces 3, for example, gets a team together. A group of Extraordinary Citizens or staff of some kind, fueled by his mastery of Prime 2 and Forces 3, make a set of plasma grenades for him to use. It's his magick at work, he's just working via the Management/HR instrument with a Hypereconomic practice.<br /> <br /> If you wanted to make that more engaging, you could make your Syndicate guy an engineer himself, and have him get directly involved in a hands-on manner.<br /> <br /> So if I were making a PC with this concept, I'd give them Crafts (Design), Technology, Hypertech, Leadership. Hands of Daedalus merit. 5 points in the Cult background, but not an actual Cult of course; this is just to represent his team of highly trained extraordinary citizens. Make Prime (or Primal Utility) the Affinity sphere, and make Wonders of all kinds their main focus as a Mage. So with Arete 3 and his team helping him, in his Sanctum, he's able to make all sorts of crazy gadgets. He works for the R&amp;D wing of a successful corporation, which is where he gets his funding, and how he fits into the Syndicate concept. He's the one meeting with boards to discuss funding and profits, their next line of products etc. etc.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:11px\"><i>You could even consider the old Wonder Spheres option presented in one of the old books (Sons of Ether Revised I think?), which makes it so that you can only use your Spheres to craft Wonders, BUT you get dots in Spheres at half price. Quite a radical choice though.</i></span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-03-31T02:20:10-07:00","id":1301254,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301211#post1301211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Your understanding of mechanics is flawed and perpetuates a major issue of &quot;Mystics can do whatever they want because Magic I don't have to explain it&quot; but not holding Technocrats to the same standards. A naked technocrat optimized for being a naked Technocrat will be at least as good or better than your Naked mystic. The idea that &quot;They always have chanting/hymns or their blood&quot; is no different than saying &quot;A technocrat always has his training and his cyberware&quot;.<br /> <br /> The only advantage mystics have is easy of surpassing Instrament. That's it. They do not have a greater access to ease of use or have instraments that are more prone to being used in combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you'll find that your understanding is flawed. Not going to entertain this discussion beyond this last reply by the way; make the most of it.<br /> <br /> Mystics are, RAW, as stated in M20, more comfortable with working in ways that push the boundaries of belief. The Hermetic KNOWS that in an ideal world he'd be able to summon fire just by wanting it to happen; the words/chants/writings all exist inside his head, writing them IRL is a crutch. So it's easy enough for the player to say &quot;I'm going to work without focus, happy to take the +3 difficulty&quot;, or &quot;this isn't how he LIKES doing magick, but he can chant the words instead of inscribing them lovingly... is that +1 difficulty, since he isn't used to it?&quot; This is absolutely in the spirit of Mage, and it isn't just saying &quot;I can do whatever because Magic I don't need to explain it&quot;. The explanation is right there; he knows in his heart that it's possible to summon fire by chanting. He's seen people doing it, he accepts it as part of his belief. It isn't how he likes to do it, but he knows that he COULD. So the naked Mystic needs to have their mouth gagged and their limbs restrained.<br /> <br /> Things are very different for our strict technomage, especially if they're a technocrat. They simply don't accept humans creating fire out of thin air, short of rare exceptions like Psionic powers. This is why M20 literally says what I'm saying:<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:16px\"><i>The second part is a bit trickier: Does your mage BELIEVE that he can do such things? Sure, maybe you\u2019ve got Forces 3 and Prime 2 on your character sheet. A Black Suit can\u2019t just snap her fingers and make fireballs appear, however. Such feats don\u2019t fit her focus \u2013 they violate her beliefs. She can\u2019t conjure fire using someone else\u2019s focus, either; if you gave her a rune-carved staff, she\u2019d try to hit someone with it, not summon a firestorm. That sort of nonsense is Reality Deviance, and so \u2013 Spheres be damned \u2013 the Black Suit could no more conjure fire with a staff than she could fart unicorns and shoot them into space.<br /> <br /> So that\u2019s where roleplaying comes in.<br /> <br /> If the answer is No, as with the Black Suit mentioned above, then think of another way to make things happen. Okay, the rune-carved staff won\u2019t work\u2026 but a lighter and some hairspray would! In the story, the agent rushes to the bathroom cabinet, grabs the Aqua Net, pulls a lighter out of her pocket, and there you go. Now your Black Suit can conjure a fireball with a Forces 3/ Prime 2 Effect, using the lighter and the spray can as a focus instrument.</i></span><br /> <br /> I recommend that you reread M20 thoroughly. Apologies if I'm coming off as patronizing, but if you're going to come for me and state that I have a flawed understanding, you're going to have to accept it when I correct you.<br /> <br /> Yes, black suits have some &quot;naked&quot; foci. That's why I... listed those foci, and spoke about how they could perform some effects even with no physical tools. But while a Mystic trapped in a room with only their own body can meditate until they feel like they're floating off into the distance (stepping sideways with Spirit 3, or teleporting to their sanctum with Correspondence 3, assuming the room isn't warded in some way) a black suit <b>SIMPLY DOES NOT BELIEVE THAT THEY CAN TELEPORT BY MEDITATING</b>. To them, meditation is a way to deepen their understanding of their own mind and body; it's what they'd use to boost their Willpower, Mental Attributes, or figure out an escape route via Entropy, or even predict likely outcomes of their imprisonment with Time 2. Black suits do not make themselves Reality Deviants. They're capable of performing Vulgar magick, but it HAS to be Vulgar magick that they believe in. And even if they have seen a Reality Deviant teleport away while meditating, he doesn't believe he can do that, since he knows he isn't a reality deviant. Agents of the NWO are committed to removing things like self-willed teleportation from reality, and replacing them with teleportation devices and similar, as per the Timetable.<br /> <br /> The black suit is not robbed of all options, they're not powerless, they're not completely stranded, but they are put in a position where they need to jury-rig a solution. Perhaps they can tear out the wiring in the walls and use Technology to craft a device that they can use as a focus, grab a guard's gun after distracting them with a Mind procedure via eye contact, use Mind procedures and meditation to make themselves immune to torture, or use Mind/Life and Martial Arts to force their body to temporarily mimic death in the hopes that they'll dispose of his body in a way where he can escape.<br /> <br /> Anyways I'm sure Likes/Replies will demonstrate that most of the people on this forum, who are very well versed in Mage mechanics, will agree with me in general. I get the impression that you feel kind of salty and really hate Mystics being able to &quot;make shit up&quot;, but it isn't really making shit up if you believe that people can do &quot;impossible&quot; things just by willing it. There are huge difficulty modifiers associated with this sort of thing. This sort of thing is very hard. Stepping sideways out of a moving truck while tied up is not easy. But Mystics can ATTEMPT it if nothing else. But a Void Engineer in the same situation simply doesn't BELIEVE that they can cross over into another dimension with sheer will alone. They think they need rare and specialized equipment, protective suits, lots of electricity to power the equipment... a Technocrat who thinks &quot;I know that mystics can do this, and those shapeshifting Deviants, so I should be able to do it with Enlightened Reasoning and Psionic Disruption&quot; might be able to do it, at the usual +3 difficulty, BUT such a Technocrat is unusual, unmutual and the Union will frown upon such activity if they become aware of it.<br /> <br /> Anyways, again, read M20 thoroughly, and try to be more polite to people who know more than you <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> And, to reiterate, you might benefit from reading this:<br /> <br /> <i>It's sometimes worth simply boiling it down to Vulgar or Coincidental when it comes to this sort of thing; one of the features listed as something to consider with Vulgar/Coincidental is Resources. Are you working with something that already exists (the taxi down the street) or something that didn't exist (a taxi you create out of thin air).<br /> <br /> If a Technocrat's explanation for blowing up a lightbulb to inflict a huge amount of damage to a building is &quot;well it has secret technological features&quot;, that's bullshit. It's just made up. That's Vulgar, simply because the internal explanation doesn't even make full sense within the Mage's paradigm; a lightbulb shattering could be coincidental, but the Mage's own internal justifications have to make sense. Just in the way that someone could just happen to develop a cold, but an Akashayana couldn't infect them by sending them an email, since that's not how their magick works, and they don't believe in it. Instead the Technocrat should actually use their Paradigm (worth remembering that while the whole union has highly standardized and formatted Practice, which makes it very easy to work together, personal Paradigm will vary, from Tech Holds All Answers to Might Makes Right and Everything is Data) to determine how they make the lightbulb explode.<br /> <br /> &quot;Well by tweaking the wavelengths traveling through space around me using my personal disruption gear, I can concentrate a lattice of electromagnetic energy around the lightbulb to blow it up&quot;. Simple. Much better than pretending something always was a certain way.<br /> <br /> This problem only really seems to come up with Mages who aren't proper scientists in and of themselves; black suits who are the agents of the technocracy. In this case you simply have to remember that NOT ALL MAGES CAN DO EVERYTHING. Sometimes Technocrats are at a significant disadvantage because they simply can't justify blowing something up or conjuring fire, they are limited to scaring people with suit/sunglasses and Mind, blasting people with a gun with forces, finding the perfect part of a building to destroy to bring the whole thing down with Matter/Entropy... you get the idea. Some Mages are limited. Some are stuck at their desk with every effect taking 5 hours because to them alchemy is this slow process. And that's fine. If you don't want your mage to be like that, design a Mage with a paradigm/practice that allows improvisation. There's a reason that Techgnosi / Scientific Mystic is an expensive merit. There are huge benefits and drawbacks to both technomancy and mysticism. Having the benefits of both is powerful.</i><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 03-31-2019, 05:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-01T09:36:30-07:00","id":1301658,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Brassfist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301245#post1301245\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you think it makes sense to play a syndicate that actually understands and works with products that they create? Like, I\u2019d like to play an entrepreneurial engineer of some sort that still can craft solutions and devices like an iterator or a progenitor when needed? What methodology would that fall into (spd doesn\u2019t count because they are all evil as hell). The NWO has Q division and there are barely any details listed for them, but I could see a macguyver type come from them. I\u2019m having trouble with figuring out what that would look like for the syndicate ?</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. One of the things about the way the Technocracy works is that the Conventions aren't completely separate ideas or styles, they are separate approaches. While they push the envelope in unique ways they are largely using the same fundamental paradigms as each other. Progenitors are the best and most focused on life sciences, but everyone can use them. The NWO is the most focused on communication and psychology, but everyone can use those.<br /> <br /> The explanation that the Syndicate needs to understand technology well enough to figure out the best ways to introduce it to market is a very effective explanation for why they would be versed in everyone elses methods. Their Q division would take the form of market research and proof of concept. Their agents wouldn't just be using it out of convenience they'd also be reporting back on potential applications and bugs.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T03:47:58-07:00","id":1301846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301211#post1301211\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Your understanding of mechanics is flawed and perpetuates a major issue of &quot;Mystics can do whatever they want because Magic I don't have to explain it&quot; but not holding Technocrats to the same standards. A naked technocrat optimized for being a naked Technocrat will be at least as good or better than your Naked mystic. The idea that &quot;They always have chanting/hymns or their blood&quot; is no different than saying &quot;A technocrat always has his training and his cyberware&quot;.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Well I'm sorry but the rules are pretty clear. It's common knowledge that Technomagic has more substantial need of tools.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Page 320, M20<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Mystic mages begin to realize that magick comes from<br /> internal excellence, not from external focus</b>. Beginning<br /> at Arete 3, a mystic mage can begin to discard the<br /> instruments she uses to focus her magickal style.<br /> Although she doesn\u2019t have to stop using her tools, she<br /> can discard one instrument per dot in Arete over the<br /> second: one instrument at Arete 3, two at Arete 4, three<br /> at Arete 5, and so on. Typically, this is the instrument<br /> she relies upon the least, which makes it the instrument<br /> she\u2019s most able to grow beyond. By Arete 9, she will have<br /> outgrown the need for instruments at all, and will thus<br /> have become an instrument of magick herself.<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Technomancers cannot discard instruments until they<br /> reach Arete 6; </b>at that point, they can set aside a single<br /> instrument. This provides a breakthrough; from Arete 7<br /> onward, a Technomancer can set aside two instruments<br /> per dot in Arete. Again, by Arete 9, the mage will have<br /> transcended the need for an external focus.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Thanks to thorough indoctrination, members of the<br /> <b>Technocracy cannot transcend their focus at all.</b> Until and<br /> unless he leaves the Technocratic Union entirely and<br /> embraces a new belief system \u2013 as the Society of Ether and<br /> Virtual Adepts have done \u2013 <b>a Technocrat remains bound<br /> to the idea that his tools allow him to do what he does</b>. At<br /> Arete 10, a Technocrat assumes that he, his instruments,<br /> and his approach to science are all one integrated whole.<br /> Technocratic oracles are essentially ghosts in the Machine:<br /> human consciousness incarnated in technology.<br /> For details, see Focus and the Arts in Chapter Ten,<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;&quot;Mystics can do whatever they want because Magic I don't have to explain it&quot; but not holding Technocrats to the same standards.&quot;<br /> <br /> Does sound a lot like<br /> <br /> &quot;<b>Mystic mages begin to realize that magick comes from internal excellence, not from external focus while Technocracy cannot transcend their focus at all. </b> <b>a Technocrat remains bound to the idea that his tools allow him to do what he does&quot;</b><br /> <br /> It's not a &quot;flawed understanding&quot; of the rules. It's THE rule. Page 320. No two way around it.<br /> <br /> Even more, your technocrat cannot always have is cyberware<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> This is the reason most technocratic rotes depends on tools so heavily and are more &quot;limited&quot; in scope.<br /> Take Iteration X revised convention book and you'll make a discovery: they only have gadget, and no rotes.<br /> If you search in all the book you will find not a single rote, only devices.<br /> <br /> <br /> This is why Convention book have so much gear and device pages, much more than a Tradition Book.<br /> <br /> It is even stated that some Convention have more gear than they have procedures.<br /> <br /> Progenitors convention books revised, page 72<br /> <br /> &quot;<b>While gear is very common in the<br /> Convention, certain Procedures make the rounds in Progenitor training</b>. These<br /> Procedures are well-kept secrets, tricks,<br /> and tools of the trade. Unlike Progenitor<br /> gear, which anyone with an Enlightened<br /> Science background can be trained to<br /> do, these Procedures require specialized<br /> knowledge to use in the field. Because<br /> of that, the devices these Procedures use<br /> are rarely given to anyone outside of the Convention.&quot;<br /> <br /> Syndicate<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <b>Like other Technocrats, Syndicate agents want reliable hypertech by their side. Procedures fail, call for<br /> exotic precursors, and risk Market Correction in strange<br /> environments</b> \u2014 and sometimes you just want to shoot<br /> someone in the face without running the numbers first.<br /> Besides, using an Extraordinary Device might act as a bit<br /> of viral marketing so that in time, hypertech becomes the<br /> standard. It happened with smartphones, didn\u2019t it? In<br /> these cases, agents use the Extraordinary Devices below. </div> </div> </div><br /> Most rotes in the convention books cannot be done without the right tool. And it can also happen that the &quot;extension&quot; of the rotes depends on the tool that was used.<br /> <br /> <br /> So yeah, most rotes that requires tool for an Euthanatos or a Hermetic can be done without them, after arete 3 the Hermetic understand that the magic staff it's just an instrument.<br /> The technocrat cannot do it. If he needs the quantum teleporter to get out of the building, he can't get out without the quantum teleporter, no matter what.<br /> Now there are some rotes that can always be done (the one that relies on training and physical actions). but no, no plasma-gun-arm-multi-cyber-use if you don't have a plasma-gun-arm-multi-cyber-use on you.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now, the thing most useful for a Technocrat in this case is &quot;mass production&quot;.<br /> The satellite with the death ray is already there in the sky, because Technocracy build it in advance. Q Division has always the multi-rocket-gun you want (if you have enough requisition) and so on. It's like if you took an entire House of Hermetics and decide that they must inscribe Mars Seals and enochian parchments all the day. That's a solution.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Let's make a practical example<br /> <br /> <br /> Hermetic rote<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Vulcan\u2019s Hammer<br /> [Forces \u2022\u2022\u2022, Life \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022, Matter \u2022\u2022\u2022]<br /> <br /> There are times when subtlety is called for. Vulcan\u2019s<br /> Hammer is not a rote for those times. This enchantment<br /> transforms a mage into a living weapon of iron and flame. The<br /> effect is most often focused through a statuette of the mage in<br /> question, sculpted from iron-rich clay and hardened under<br /> intense heat. Over the course of working the clay, the magescribes and then kneads her True Name into it 10 separate times,<br /> thus concealing the truth of her identity within it and making<br /> the statuette a conduit through which her own living Pattern<br /> may be affected. (Understandably, an Order mage does well to<br /> carefully protect the focus for this Effect, as it does contain<br /> information about her innermost being, through which all<br /> manner of deleterious magics might be worked against her.) </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Given this rule &quot;<br /> <br /> \u2022 <b>Mystic mages begin to realize that magick comes from<br /> internal excellence, not from external focus</b>. Beginning<br /> at Arete 3, a mystic mage can begin to discard the<br /> instruments she uses to focus her magickal style.&quot;<br /> <br /> A mage with enough Arete will not need the statuette or the True name inscribing.<br /> <br /> <br /> on the other hand let's take a Technocratic procedure<br /> <br /> &quot;Jung\u2019s Trick (\u2022\u2022\u2022 Mind, \u2022\u2022 Data)<br /> Dreams are shortcuts through the labyrinth of the<br /> mind. Most discount their significance, but those who<br /> do so only discount them due to their own inability to<br /> properly translate the symbols and codes of dreams. Jung\u2019s<br /> Trick requires the agent to research a subject\u2019s dreams.<br /> At least three such dreams are necessary. Often, this<br /> means probing the subject\u2019s mind or using hypnosis. A<br /> dream diary could work, as could a bestselling vampire<br /> romance novel written based on a young woman\u2019s own<br /> dreamscapes. It would not apply to fan fiction inspired<br /> by the novel, however.<br /> Once properly examined, the agent creates a computer<br /> algorithm to better understand the subject\u2019s subconscious.<br /> Then, using wireless protocols, the computer can detect and<br /> interpret dream emanations. Functionally, this requires the<br /> subject\u2019s player to make a declaration of one important thing<br /> she aims for her character to do the following day. If at all<br /> possible, she must attempt to do that thing.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> By this rule<br /> <br /> &quot;<b>Technocracy cannot transcend their focus at all.</b> Until and<br /> unless he leaves the Technocratic Union entirely and<br /> embraces a new belief system \u2013 as the Society of Ether and<br /> Virtual Adepts have done \u2013 <b>a Technocrat remains bound<br /> to the idea that his tools allow him to do what he does</b>.&quot;<br /> <br /> The NWO agent will always, need three dreams and a computer algorithm, no matter what.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> If Voormas is naked in the forest and meet John Courage, who is also naked, Voormas can do every spell it comes to his mind, with no limitation.<br /> Summoning angels? Firestorm? Freezing time? Teleport? Create a double? Summoning an horde of animals? Becoming a 10 meters Avatar of war? He can do it, he doesn't need tools.<br /> John Courage can only do what his naked body let him do. Super Martial arts, mental defenses, speech tricks and so on. But no plasma ray, no teleporting, no death ray from above and so on. If he has the enanchement background than he could have some cyberware, but if he doesn't have that background, then no no, he doesn't have a computer in his head or a shotgun in his arm.<br /> <br /> <br /> Now let's say that Caeron Mustai is naked in a forest, and meet a master of Iteration X, his body full of cyberware that allows him to do a lot of things more than John Courage could.<br /> He is still weaker than Mustai, because naked Mustai doesn't have weakness or limitations, while Iteration X master has the following problems.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Page 657 M20<br /> <br /> <br /> Permanent Paradox: As mentioned earlier, <b>each dot in the<br /> Enhancement Background bestows a point of permanent<br /> Paradox. </b>Enlightened characters add those points to their<br /> Paradox Effect backlashes. UnEnlightened characters with<br /> cybernetic implants suffer weird quirks and chronic health<br /> issues (see Genetic Flaws, pp. 648-651) and contribute<br /> to the overall backlash total when an allied mage suffers<br /> a Paradox backlash. Yes, this means that an Enlightened<br /> Technocrat surrounded by unEnlightened cyborgs can<br /> be really fucked if Paradox kicks in.<br /> A large Paradox backlash (10 points or more) drains all<br /> of the Primal Energy out of the cybernetic devices and<br /> kicks them offline until someone finds a new source of<br /> energy for them.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Extraction: Cybernetic enhancements are machines, and<br /> machines can be removed. If a cyborg character winds<br /> up Incapacitated or otherwise immobilized, he might get<br /> chopped if his captors are skillful or ruthless enough to do<br /> so. In game terms, a skillful character has several dots in<br /> Biotech, Hypertech, Medicine, or Science (Cybernetics).<br /> An unskilled character could just use a chainsaw.<br /> Assume that a character whose cybernetics have been<br /> ganked loses one permanent health level for each dot of<br /> Enhancements. Three dots\u2019 worth of cybernetics, then,<br /> would inflict three health levels\u2019 worth of damage if<br /> removed. Such removal inflicts aggravated damage that<br /> cannot be healed without significant restoration magick<br /> or medical treatment. Certain organs or improvements,<br /> of course, might prove immediately fatal if removed. As<br /> one might imagine, the threat of extraction keeps most<br /> Technocratic cyborgs in line.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Magickal Vandalism: <b>Despite the Technocracy\u2019s best<br /> efforts, the awful power of the Spheres allows a skillful<br /> mage to take cyborgs apart. A vulgar Life 3/ Forces 2/<br /> Prime 2 Effect can disrupt cybernetic devices if the mage<br /> knows what she\u2019s doing \u2013 in game terms, the four Traits<br /> listed directly above this entry. If that mage rolls five<br /> successes or more, the poor cyborg goes offline until a<br /> repair tech can sort things out. Quite often, he remains<br /> conscious even while trapped in his malfunctioning<br /> body \u2013 a nightmarish fate that haunts each cybernetic<br /> Ascension Warrior.</b><br /> <br /> \u2022 Implant Rejection: An even more gruesome fate involves<br /> the wholesale rejection of cybernetic devices. <b>A vulgar<br /> Life 3 (or Life 3/ Matter 2) spell inflicts two additional<br /> health levels in aggravated damage if the spell gets past<br /> a cyborg\u2019s countermagick. The cyborg cannot soak this<br /> damage in any way. </b>A single assault that deals out six<br /> health levels or more actually rips the cybernetics out<br /> of the character\u2019s body \u2013 a brutally effective tactic when<br /> faced with enhanced antagonists.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So even with integrated foci, Technocratics mages have problems given by the nature of the tools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12142,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","user":"Undead rabbit","date":". 04-02-2019, 07:30 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","name":"Undead rabbit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T05:11:55-07:00","id":1301865,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"12142\" data-vbnamecard=\"12142\">Undead rabbit</a> Largely agreed, and well written! Have a like.<br /> <br /> I think you might be downplaying the power of cyborgs a little bit however. Primium Countermeasures are a real jackpot when it comes to a direct fight with another Mage, who has to find a workaround to inflict damage. And they can probably inflict *more* damage by turning the floor into lava or making spirits melt you, BUT the key factor is that the vulgar &quot;now your cybernetic bones are going to pop out of your skin&quot; effects will always be resisted by the Primium. HIT Mark Vs all have Primium 5 these days, and so do the Mark Xs, just off the top of my head.<br /> <br /> The Paradox issue is a significant downside yes, but I think it's at least partially counteracted by the fact that most Technocratic procedures are Coincidental in most parts of the world... that is, Aggravated bullets sprayed everywhere as opposed to energy rays of course.<br /> <br /> But in general we do agree on the rules and that's good. Technocrats are more dependent on their Tools in many ways.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-02-2019, 08:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T05:49:04-07:00","id":1301872,"title":"","content":"Indeed Primium is good, but is VERY costly too.<br /> <br /> It doesn't come free, it must be bought after you bought the cyberware.<br /> <br /> 4 background points buys 2 dice of countermagic.<br /> 6 background points buys 3<br /> 8 background points buys 4<br /> 10 background points buys 5<br /> <br /> Let's say you buy an implanted plasma cannon and just 2 dot of countermagic<br /> <br /> it's 13 dot worth of background.<br /> Which is a LOT.<br /> <br /> <br /> Or let's keep it to a bare minimumOnline access + 2 dice of countermagic<br /> <br /> it's still 6 background points.<br /> <br /> Even more Primium is a background lore-costrained.<br /> <br /> M20 page 650<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> To foil the Arts of Reality Deviants, the Technocracy has<br /> developed a microweave modification that implants flexible<br /> Primium underneath the skin of its valued agents. Even after two<br /> decades of experimentation, no other faction has successfully<br /> duplicated or countered the Union\u2019s formula for Primium,<br /> which suggests that Technocracy scientists maintain a constant<br /> process of innovation and perfection with that material and its<br /> many applications. <b>This, of course, renders it very expensive;<br /> the Union doesn\u2019t waste such resources on just anyone, and<br /> it expects great things and unswerving loyalty from an agent<br /> implanted with such modifications.</b><br /> <br /> System: A Primium-implanted character has several dice of<br /> innate countermagick. Four points buy two dice of protection,<br /> six points buy three dice, eight points buy four, and 10 points<br /> secure five. <b>Even in the 21st century, a character MUST be a<br /> Technocrat in good standing in order to enjoy the benefits of<br /> Primium. If an apparently disloyal cyborg earns an extraction<br /> penalty, this mod will be the first thing the technicians remove</b>. </div> </div> </div><br /> Besides Iteration X is not the only one who knows how to create Primium, Sons of Ethers have access to the rote too, same for Chelestial Chorus and the Order of Hermes as of Revised (after the first was joined by the Templars, and the latter by the Solficati, who both knew how to create Primium).<br /> \u00ec","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12142,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","user":"Undead rabbit","date":". 04-02-2019, 09:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12142-undead-rabbit","name":"Undead rabbit","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"888"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T08:13:37-07:00","id":1301914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Undead rabbit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301872#post1301872\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Indeed Primium is good, but is VERY costly too.<br /> <br /> It doesn't come free, it must be bought after you bought the cyberware.<br /> <br /> 4 background points buys 2 dice of countermagic.<br /> 6 background points buys 3<br /> 8 background points buys 4<br /> 10 background points buys 5<br /> <br /> Let's say you buy an implanted plasma cannon and just 2 dot of countermagic<br /> <br /> it's 13 dot worth of background.<br /> Which is a LOT.<br /> <br /> Besides Iteration X is not the only one who knows how to create Primium, Sons of Ethers have access to the rote too, same for Chelestial Chorus and the Order of Hermes as of Revised (after the first was joined by the Templars, and the latter by the Solficati, who both knew how to create Primium).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you've misunderstood the Enhancements Background; look at the ratings of the Background.<br /> <br /> X No special modifications.<br /> \u2022 +1 Attribute dot, or 3 points for Devices. One Paradox point or Genetic Flaw.<br /> \u2022\u2022 +2 Attribute dots, or 6 points for Devices. Two Paradox points or Genetic Flaws.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 +3 Attribute dots, or 9 points for Devices. Three Paradox points or Genetic Flaws.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 +4 Attribute dots, or 12 points for Devices. Four Paradox points or Genetic Flaws.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022 +5 Attribute dots, or 15 points for Devices. Five Paradox points or Genetic Flaws.<br /> <br /> So for &quot;only&quot; 4 points in the Enhancement background (8 freebies, for example) you get 12 points of Devices. 12 points covers 5 dice of Primium (10 points) and the endoskeleton reinforcements (2 points) which give two bonus health levels and are needed for extensive modifications. With 5 points in the Enhancement Background you then have 3 points to put into other cool Devices, with Online Access being a personal favourite.<br /> <br /> So it's a lot less expensive than it would be if you needed to pay for everything with freebies directly, instead of buying them indirectly via the Enhancement background.<br /> <br /> As for the Traditions being able to make Primium... there's no M20 content that mentions, states or references that. As far as I'm aware &quot;everyone has Primium&quot; (even if to a very limited extent) was Revised era, and has basically been written out of M20. So basically it still exists if the ST likes the way Revised handled it. I run it as Primium being uniquely Technocratic in the sense that the substance is emblematic of the Union. M20 also explicitly says that a character MUST be a Union operative in good standing in order to enjoy the benefits of Primium, which means that others can't access it RAW in M20.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-02-2019, 11:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T09:42:47-07:00","id":1301930,"title":"","content":"Also, another thing, <br /> <br /> Even if the traditions can make Primium... its a whole lot less than the technocracy. The Technocracy's themes are The Man, Control, and Infrastructure. Any Primium you get is going to be a lot more expensive than in the technocracy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12952-accelerator","name":"Accelerator","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3232"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-02T12:51:30-07:00","id":1301979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Accelerator</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301930#post1301930\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, another thing,<br /> <br /> Even if the traditions can make Primium... its a whole lot less than the technocracy. The Technocracy's themes are The Man, Control, and Infrastructure. Any Primium you get is going to be a lot more expensive than in the technocracy.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, and on top of that the default rules for having Enhancements outside of the Technocratic Union is to take extra flaws since you didn't have a dedicated team of full-time specialists performing your implants.<br /> <br /> &quot;Non-Technocratic Cybernetics: Although other factions have cybernetic adaptations (notably the Society of Ether, Virtual Adepts, certain techno-wizards, and some Nephandi), the most efficient and reliable enhancements come from the Technocratic Union. That Union has been refining such advances in a systematic form for over a century, so the more idiosyncratic inventions of rival technomancers can\u2019t even come close.<br /> <br /> As a rule, assume that a non-Technocracy character with cybernetic implants suffers from one Genetic Flaw Trait for every dot in the Enhancements Background. These Flaws reflect the unstable nature of cybernetic innovations outside the specialized disciplines of the Technocratic Union\u2019s laboratories.&quot;<br /> <br /> Personally I'm not a big fan of this rule since it discourages people from doing something really cool like playing a Technoshaman with Enhancements 1 who gradually gains more Enhancements throughout the chronicle... after all, that's going to mean 5 genetic flaws, so you'll be an uncanny mess. I'd revise it to one genetic flaw regardless of the points in the Background, and let the player take something like &quot;they're far FAR heavier than they should be&quot; or &quot;they have to eat twice as much as a normal person and this stands out&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-02-2019, 03:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-03T07:42:42-07:00","id":1302206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301979#post1301979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Personally I'm not a big fan of this rule since it discourages people from doing something really cool like playing a Technoshaman with Enhancements 1 who gradually gains more Enhancements throughout the chronicle...&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>Me neither. Also it's wording discourages mystical enhancements like Nuada's hand and lots of other ancient prosthetics made by smith gods. In that department the Traditions should have much more experience<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-04T21:19:46-07:00","id":1302734,"title":"","content":"Also, magical tattoos ought to count as Enhancements. More generally, an \u201cEnhancement\u201d is what happens when a mage decides to create a Relic (a living Wonder), and the foundation for said Relic is <i>you</i>.<br /> <br /> (In MRev, specifically Guide to the Traditions, the Traditions' counterpart to Enhancements were called Blessings. Then Phil got ahold of it, missed the point of Blessings, and completely rewrote them for M20.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div style=\"margin-left:40px\"> <div align=\"center\">My hacks: <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwtrin\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#0000FF\">Trinity Continuum</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwscion\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#008000\">Scion</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwExalt\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#DAA520\">Exalted</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwcwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#800000\">World of Darkness</span></a>, <a href=\"http://tinyurl.com/dwnwod\" target=\"_blank\"><span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Chronicles of Darkness</span></a></div> </div>","edited":{"userid":421,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","user":"Dataweaver","date":". 04-05-2019, 12:27 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/421-dataweaver","name":"Dataweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar421_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8420"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-05T20:08:51-07:00","id":1302987,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301914#post1301914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As for the Traditions being able to make Primium... there's no M20 content that mentions, states or references that. As far as I'm aware &quot;everyone has Primium&quot; (even if to a very limited extent) was Revised era, and has basically been written out of M20. So basically it still exists if the ST likes the way Revised handled it. I run it as Primium being uniquely Technocratic in the sense that the substance is emblematic of the Union. M20 also explicitly says that a character MUST be a Union operative in good standing in order to enjoy the benefits of Primium, which means that others can't access it RAW in M20.</div> </div> </div> </div>Even Revised Era had Primium only available to Technocratic offshoots. and the Children of Knowledge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2715-lian","name":"Lian","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5218"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-06T20:44:24-07:00","id":1303238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>11twiggins</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1301254#post1301254\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mystics are, RAW, as stated in M20, more comfortable with working in ways that push the boundaries of belief. The Hermetic KNOWS that in an ideal world he'd be able to summon fire just by wanting it to happen; the words/chants/writings all exist inside his head, writing them IRL is a crutch. So it's easy enough for the player to say &quot;I'm going to work without focus, happy to take the +3 difficulty&quot;, or &quot;this isn't how he LIKES doing magick, but he can chant the words instead of inscribing them lovingly... is that +1 difficulty, since he isn't used to it?&quot; This is absolutely in the spirit of Mage, and it isn't just saying &quot;I can do whatever because Magic I don't need to explain it&quot;. The explanation is right there; he knows in his heart that it's possible to summon fire by chanting. He's seen people doing it, he accepts it as part of his belief. It isn't how he likes to do it, but he knows that he COULD. So the naked Mystic needs to have their mouth gagged and their limbs restrained.</div> </div> </div> </div>Heck, I've thought of how a Hermetic could cast spells - with Instruments - even tied up and gagged. He uses his Mind Palace (the use of which can be either Meditation or Thought Forms).<br /> <br /> The Hermetic visualizes his Mind Palace, and specifically a chamber within that conceptual space dedicated to ritual work. I mean Dedication in the mystical sense. When he was designing his Mind Palace, long before he ever got into this situation, he took time to set aside space for a room that is built from the ground up to mimic a ritual space he had in reality.<br /> <br /> When he casts a spell with it, he visualizes the space, and all the ritual implements, and acts out the intricate series of steps needed to cast an effect. Not with any tools he might have dedicated beforehand to a task so he could employ it quickly, but in the &quot;slow way&quot;, going back to first principles of Hermetic High Ritual. All the physical tools he made are shortcuts, so he could cast specific Effects more quickly when he needed them. The core of Hermeticism - the groundwork upon which the whole Art is based - is the slow, methodical work he must now return to, but in his imagination.<br /> <br /> Casting an Effect with a Mind Palace is by no means fast, easy, or <i>convenient</i>.<br /> <br /> Even standing in his actual ritual room would be faster, and less mentally draining. Because the Hermetic must keep track of the space, the ritual implements, the altar on which these implements sit, the intricate designs on the floor, and the litany of chants he must mentally recite. Even elementary rituals might take significantly longer to pull off in a Mind Palace, because he does not have the benefit of space and implements and designs and chanted words that exist independent of himself. He must keep track of it all, or he'll not only fail, but potentially fail spectacularly. Imagine if a wizard tried to summon a spirit, but didn't draw the protective circles correctly. This is the stakes a Hermetic faces when trying to visualize their way to magick. To do so requires concentration, memorization, the ability to multitask, and freedom from distraction.<br /> <br /> It's not easy. It's not fast. In every way, it's less convenient than having any number of other Instruments. And it requires the Hermetic to be trained in that kind of deeply mental exercise. But it can be done, when necessary. If it were me, I'd count that as &quot;casting <i>with</i> Instruments&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-07T01:16:37-07:00","id":1303264,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1300186-technocracy-spontaneous-infrastructure-or-technocratic-magic?p=1303238#post1303238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not easy. It's not fast. In every way, it's less convenient than having any number of other Instruments. And it requires the Hermetic to be trained in that kind of deeply mental exercise. But it can be done, when necessary. If it were me, I'd count that as &quot;casting <i>with</i> Instruments&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's casting with Instruments, however their Meditation / Lucid Dreaming need to be up to the task. If they're trying to use a level 3 effect then they need 3+ in the relevant skills. Demesne would also help of course, since then you're doing the ritual in a &quot;Real&quot; place rather than &quot;just doing it inside your head&quot; (although some Mages would, naturally, get extremely angry at the use of the word &quot;just&quot; there).<br /> <br /> It's the sort of focus that might also demand a point of Willpower. But yes, really cool and fitting. And a Black Suit could do a similar thing (for example, do their full martial arts routine, or self-improvement mantra, inside their head) it's just that the results would be improved Attributes and clever answers to the situation at hand, not a summoned Spirit Familiar or teleportation away from danger.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}